# Get Started

This notebook will guide you through the first steps of using the PSFramework module.

## Prerequisites

To make full use of this notebook, open it in Visual Studio Code with the PowerShell and Polyglot extensions installed.

To use the PSFramework PowerShell module, version 3.0 of PowerShell needs to be installed as well.

## Installation

The PSFramework module can be installed from the PowerShell Gallery. To do so, run the following PowerShell command:

#!pwsh

Install-Module PSFramework

#!markdown

### Installation prompts

Either way, the installation may prompt you for questions / confirmations about core PowerShell configurations. You need to either accept or approve all the prompts for the module to work correctly. The prompts usually require you to enter "y" (for yes) or "a" (for "yes to all").

#!markdown

## Importing the module

Once the module is installed, it can be imported into the current PowerShell session. To do so, run the following command.

#!pwsh

Import-Module PSFramework

#!markdown

## Using the module

The PSFramework module includes several areas of functionality, among them
- Advanced message & logging functionality
- Manage scripts & modules using DSC
- Simple Tab Completion
- Runspace-safe development
- PowerShell Scheduled tasks

As part of this notebook on getting started, we will only take a quick look into the first area around logging. Other notebooks may cover this area and the others in more detail.

### A quick peek into logging

With PSFramework, you have the powerful `Write-PSFMessage` command available. This gives you advanced messaging and logging capabilities.

Instead of

#!pwsh

Write-Verbose "logging something"

#!markdown

you can now use

#!pwsh

Write-PSFMessage "logging something"

#!markdown

Both commands write the message to verbose. However, `Write-PSFMessage` also writes to a log file. In addition, once a log file gets too old or too large, it is automatically cleaned up.

To find the folder where the log files are placed, you can use `Get-PSFConfigValue` command (don't worry about the `psframework.logging.filesystem.logpath` for now; this will be explained in another place):

#!pwsh

Get-PSFConfigValue -FullName psframework.logging.filesystem.logpath

#!markdown

Combined with the standard [Get-ChildItem](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.management/get-childitem) command, you can use it to list the log files in the folder:

#!pwsh

Get-PSFConfigValue -FullName psframework.logging.filesystem.logpath | Get-ChildItem

#!markdown

You could now use [Get-Content](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.management/get-content) to inspect the log files. PSFramework however has the dedicated `Get-PSFMessage` command for that.

#!pwsh

Get-PSFMessage

#!markdown

If you are wondering what other logging capabilities PSFramework provides, take a look at the [logging system documentation](https://psframework.org/documentation/documents/psframework/logging.html). Other notebooks may cover these capabilities as well.

#!markdown

## Learn more about the module

Now that you know how to install, configure and run a simple command of the module, it is time to learn how you can learn more about the module.
Not every command will have a notebook like this to explain it. However, each command has documentation that can help you learn how to use it.
To view the documentation for the `Write-PSFMessage` command, run the following [Get-Help](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.core/get-help) command:

#!pwsh

Get-Help Write-PSFMessage

#!markdown

As the `REMARKS` section at the end says, you can add some switches to the `Get-Help` command to get additional information. One of the most useful is the `-Examples` switch that shows you how the command can be used for different scenarios.

#!pwsh

Get-Help Write-PSFMessage -Examples

#!markdown

# Next steps

Now that you have successfully run your first cmdlet, you can continue with other notebooks in this folder to learn more about the PSFramework module and how to use it.

Take a look at [Learn commands](Learn-Commands.ipynb) to get an introduction to other cmdlets of the module.
